DEFAULT=$HOME/Maildir/
PMDIR=$HOME/.procmail
LOGFILE=$PMDIR/log
LOG="
"
MAILDIR=$HOME/Maildir       # Make sure this directory exists!

# Procmail runs any action line containing shell metacharacters via
# $SHELL -c. The compiled user rules below must never reach a shell, so
# SHELL points at false: the legitimate pipes in this pipeline
# (push-enqueue here and in compiled rules) contain no metacharacters and
# are exec'd directly, bypassing SHELL entirely, while a value that
# somehow smuggled metacharacters past the rule compiler would exec
# /usr/bin/false instead of a shell. docs/1.x/user-mail-rules-plan.md,
# "Sandbox the recipient user".
SHELL=/usr/bin/false

# Procmail processes this file's /etc/procmailrc copy BEFORE setuiding to
# the recipient, so without this every recipe below - including the
# compiled user rules the include pulls in - would run as root and write
# root-owned Maildir files the recipient's own dovecot session cannot
# read (observed: root:mail 0600 folder deliveries, and a root-owned
# ~/.procmail/log that then rejects the recipient's appends). DROPPRIVS
# is procmail's purpose-built switch for exactly this: drop to the
# recipient here, before the first recipe. The push-spool drain already
# accepts recipient-owned signal files, so the push path is unaffected.
DROPPRIVS=yes

:0:
*^X-Mailer: Outlook Express 3.14159
IN-spam/

# User-defined mail rules (docs/1.x/user-mail-rules-plan.md): compiled
# server-side into /etc/procmail-user/<user>.rc, one file per user, by
# compile-user-rules.py on the reconfigure loop. Placed after the system
# spam rule and BEFORE the push recipe so a rule that consumes the
# message decides its push: procmail stops at the first delivering
# recipe, so deleted mail never buzzes, and filed mail gets its wake
# signal from a compiler-emitted push-enqueue carrying the destination
# folder. The CABALRULESDONE guard makes the include run exactly once
# whichever rcfile copy runs first (same pattern as PUSH_ENQUEUED below).
:0
* ! CABALRULESDONE ?? yes
{
  CABALRULESDONE=yes
  INCLUDERC=/etc/procmail-user/$LOGNAME.rc
}

# Push notifications (docs/0.11.x/push-notifications.md): enqueue a
# content-free wake signal as a delivery side effect. The :0c carbon-copy
# flag means delivery continues unconditionally, and the script itself is
# best-effort (always exits 0), so a slow or unreachable SQS can never
# block mail flow. Placed after the spam rule on purpose: a message that
# rule files away never reaches this recipe, so junk does not buzz phones.
#
# This same file is installed at /etc/procmailrc AND copied to each user's
# ~/.procmailrc (sync-users.sh), and procmail processes both in one run.
# The PUSH_ENQUEUED variable survives the /etc -> $HOME rcfile transition,
# so the guard makes the enqueue fire exactly once whichever copy runs
# first.
:0c
* ! PUSH_ENQUEUED ?? yes
| /usr/local/bin/push-enqueue.sh "$LOGNAME" "INBOX"
PUSH_ENQUEUED=yes

# Pending decorations from continuing user rules
# (docs/1.x/rules-composition-and-custom-flags-plan.md, decisions 3 and
# 6): a decorate-only rule armed PENDING_F/PENDING_S (system flags)
# and/or PENDING_KW (custom-flag slots), and no terminal rule claimed
# the message, so it must land in the inbox WITH its decorations -
# which the implicit $DEFAULT delivery below cannot set. The condition
# never matches unless a compiled rule set a PENDING_* variable, so
# undecorated mail keeps the exact procmail-native fallback it has
# today; if the helper (or its append drain, for keywords) fails, the
# message still falls through to $DEFAULT, undecorated but delivered.
DFLAGS=$PENDING_F$PENDING_S
PENDINGANY=${DFLAGS}${PENDING_KW}

# The PENDINGTRIED guard makes the attempt fire exactly once
# whichever rcfile copy runs first (same pattern as PUSH_ENQUEUED
# above): if the helper fails here, the ~/.procmailrc pass must NOT
# spool a second attempt - the drain may still complete the first
# one, and a retried spool is how one message became several
# decorated inbox copies.
:0w
* PENDINGANY ?? .
* ! PENDINGTRIED ?? yes
| /usr/local/bin/cabal-maildir-deliver.sh "$MAILDIR" "$DFLAGS" "INBOX" "$PENDING_KW"
PENDINGTRIED=yes
